    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with a high-ceiling core, anchored by **Yordan Alvarez** (82.58) and **Junior Caminero** (80.71). **Yordan Alvarez** benefits from an elite xwOBA of .476 and a favorable matchup against the right-handed Yesavage, while **Junior Caminero** sees a massive 14% boost due to park factors, cementing their status as the offensive engines. **Kyle Schwarber** (76.87) and **Trea Turner** (76.02) follow closely behind; both leverage the soft 4.49 xERA of Palmquist, with their leadoff and two-hole assignments providing the necessary volume to capitalize on high-scoring potential. **Lars Nootbaar** remains a strong play at 67.77, provided his projected leadoff spot holds, as the matchup against V\u00e1squez offers a significant 19% SP skill advantage.\n\nThe optimizer\u2019s most difficult exclusion was **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (47.32), who missed the cut despite a baseline projection of 60.83. While his leadoff speed is highly valued, the severe penalty against the elite Skubal (2.67 xERA) and a detrimental 15% platoon factor against the left-handed starter forced him to the pine. Similarly, **Dillon Dingler** (39.14) fell just below the Zebras' 40.0 floor, as his platoon disadvantage against Hancock compounded with a weak park environment to negate any speed-based upside. \n\nRisks for today center largely on the **Lars Nootbaar** and **Pete Crow-Armstrong** lineup volatility; should the confirmed lineups shift, we lose significant certainty in those critical leadoff slots. While **Christian Yelich** (54.20) and **Nelson Velazquez** (48.11) face a difficult challenge against the sub-3.50 xERA of Jones and Weathers, their platoon advantages keep them in the marginal-to-strong start range. With **Kazuma Okamoto** (59.90) and **Caleb Durbin** (57.95) providing steady production, the Zebras are well-positioned to maintain our competitive edge in SB and counting categories, provided we navigate the defensive efficiency of the opposing arms.",
